  "live_records": {
    "q-143283": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143283",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{40}∼N(12,{20}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $40$ from a normal population, calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}<11).$\n\n*Hint: A value table of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3783\\,$ | $\\,0.3745\\,$ | $\\,0.3707\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4168\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4562\\,$ | $\\,0.4522\\,$ | $\\,0.4483\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(12,\\frac{{20}^{2}}{40}) \\\\  & ∼N(12,10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<11).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<11) & =P(Z<\\frac{11-12}{\\sqrt{10}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.32) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.32)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.32)=0.3745.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<11) & =Φ(-0.32) \\\\  & =0.3745.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
