    "q-143293": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143293",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{64}∼N(-14,{16}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $64$ from a normal population, calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>-14.86).$\n\n*Hint: A value table of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ | $\\,0.2981\\,$ | $\\,0.2946\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3372\\,$ | $\\,0.3336\\,$ | $\\,0.3300\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3745\\,$ | $\\,0.3707\\,$ | $\\,0.3669\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(-14,\\frac{{16}^{2}}{64}) \\\\  & ∼N(-14,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>-14.86).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>-14.86) & =P(Z>\\frac{-14.86-(-14)}{\\sqrt{4}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>-0.43).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-0.43) & =1-P(Z\\le -0.43) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.43).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.43)=0.3336.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>-14.86) & =1-Φ(-0.43) \\\\  & =1-0.3336 \\\\  & =0.6664.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
